# EKS: What to Learn Next — and Why You Need It

We already created an EKS cluster and deployed a microservice.
That setup works — but it has real problems in production.

This document explains **what those problems are**, and **how each next step solves them**, with actual commands you can run.

---

## The Problem with Our Basic Setup

Here is what we built:

```
Internet
   │
   ▼
LoadBalancer (1 per service)   ← costs ~$20/month each
   │
   ▼
Service → Pods (my-microservice)
```

This works for one service. But real apps have many services:

```
api-service          → needs its own LoadBalancer  ($20/month)
auth-service         → needs its own LoadBalancer  ($20/month)
payment-service      → needs its own LoadBalancer  ($20/month)
notification-service → needs its own LoadBalancer  ($20/month)
```

That's **$80/month just for load balancers**, before any EC2 cost.

On top of that:
- Nodes never auto-scale when traffic spikes
- YAML files are copy-pasted everywhere, messy to manage
- Pods use AWS services (S3, RDS) with hardcoded secrets — a security risk

The 4 things below fix these problems one by one.

---

## 1. Ingress + AWS Load Balancer Controller

### The Problem It Solves

With `type: LoadBalancer`, every single service gets its own AWS load balancer.
That is expensive and hard to manage.

**Ingress** is like a smart traffic cop sitting in front of ALL your services.
One load balancer, one public URL — traffic is routed to the right service based on the path.

```
Before (expensive):
  api.cashify.com     → LoadBalancer A → api-service
  auth.cashify.com    → LoadBalancer B → auth-service
  pay.cashify.com     → LoadBalancer C → payment-service

After (with Ingress):
  One ALB (Application Load Balancer)
  ├── /api/*    → api-service
  ├── /auth/*   → auth-service
  └── /pay/*    → payment-service
```

One load balancer. One bill. Infinite services behind it.

### Install AWS Load Balancer Controller

The AWS Load Balancer Controller is the thing that makes `Ingress` actually create an ALB on AWS.

**Step 1: Create an IAM policy for the controller**
```bash
# Download the policy document
curl -o iam_policy.json https://raw.githubusercontent.com/kubernetes-sigs/aws-load-balancer-controller/v2.7.1/docs/install/iam_policy.json

# Create the policy in AWS
aws iam create-policy \
  --policy-name AWSLoadBalancerControllerIAMPolicy \
  --policy-document file://iam_policy.json
```

**Step 2: Create a service account with that policy (IRSA — explained in Section 4)**
```bash
ACCOUNT_ID=$(aws sts get-caller-identity --query Account --output text)

eksctl create iamserviceaccount \
  --cluster=demo-test \
  --namespace=kube-system \
  --name=aws-load-balancer-controller \
  --attach-policy-arn=arn:aws:iam::${ACCOUNT_ID}:policy/AWSLoadBalancerControllerIAMPolicy \
  --approve \
  --region ap-south-1
```

**Step 3: Install the controller via Helm**
```bash
helm repo add eks https://aws.github.io/eks-charts
helm repo update

helm install aws-load-balancer-controller eks/aws-load-balancer-controller \
  --namespace kube-system \
  --set clusterName=demo-test \
  --set serviceAccount.create=false \
  --set serviceAccount.name=aws-load-balancer-controller
```

**Step 4: Verify it is running**
```bash
kubectl get deployment -n kube-system aws-load-balancer-controller
# Should show READY 2/2
```

### Now Write an Ingress Instead of LoadBalancer

Change your Service from `type: LoadBalancer` to `type: ClusterIP` (internal only):

```yaml
# service.yaml
apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata:
  name: my-microservice
spec:
  type: ClusterIP          # ← changed from LoadBalancer
  selector:
    app: my-microservice
  ports:
  - port: 80
    targetPort: 3000
```

Add a new `ingress.yaml`:

```yaml
apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: Ingress
metadata:
  name: my-ingress
  annotations:
    alb.ingress.kubernetes.io/scheme: internet-facing
    alb.ingress.kubernetes.io/target-type: ip
spec:
  ingressClassName: alb           # ← correct modern way (kubernetes.io/ingress.class annotation is deprecated since K8s 1.18)
  rules:
  - http:
      paths:
      - path: /api
        pathType: Prefix
        backend:
          service:
            name: my-microservice
            port:
              number: 80
      - path: /auth
        pathType: Prefix
        backend:
          service:
            name: auth-service    # another service behind same ALB
            port:
              number: 80
```

```bash
kubectl apply -f service.yaml
kubectl apply -f ingress.yaml

# Get the single ALB address (may take 2-3 min)
kubectl get ingress my-ingress
```

Now ALL your services share one load balancer. Adding a new service = just add a new `path` entry in the Ingress. No new load balancer created.

