## 2. Karpenter — Smart Automatic Node Scaling

### The Problem It Solves

In our basic setup, we have 2 nodes, always running, always billing — even at 3 AM when there is zero traffic.

Also, when traffic suddenly spikes and pods can't fit on 2 nodes, they just stay in `Pending` state forever. Nothing auto-scales.

**Karpenter** watches for `Pending` pods and automatically adds the right EC2 node to fit them. When traffic drops, it removes the node. You pay for exactly what you use.

```
Without Karpenter:
  2 nodes running 24/7 = fixed cost
  Spike? → pods stuck Pending, users see errors

With Karpenter:
  Low traffic  → 1 small node
  Normal       → 2 medium nodes
  Spike        → 5 large nodes (added in ~60 seconds)
  Night        → back to 1 small node
  You pay only for what's actually used
```

### Install Karpenter

**Step 1: Set environment variables**
```bash
CLUSTER_NAME=demo-test
AWS_REGION=ap-south-1
ACCOUNT_ID=$(aws sts get-caller-identity --query Account --output text)
```

**Step 2: Create Karpenter IAM roles** (two roles needed)

```bash
# --- Role 1: Node Role (EC2 nodes use this to join the cluster) ---
aws iam create-role \
  --role-name KarpenterNodeRole-${CLUSTER_NAME} \
  --assume-role-policy-document '{
    "Version": "2012-10-17",
    "Statement": [{"Effect":"Allow","Principal":{"Service":"ec2.amazonaws.com"},"Action":"sts:AssumeRole"}]
  }'

for policy in AmazonEKSWorkerNodePolicy AmazonEKS_CNI_Policy AmazonEC2ContainerRegistryReadOnly; do
  aws iam attach-role-policy \
    --role-name KarpenterNodeRole-${CLUSTER_NAME} \
    --policy-arn arn:aws:iam::aws:policy/${policy}
done

# Create an instance profile so EC2 can use this role
aws iam create-instance-profile --instance-profile-name KarpenterNodeRole-${CLUSTER_NAME}
aws iam add-role-to-instance-profile \
  --instance-profile-name KarpenterNodeRole-${CLUSTER_NAME} \
  --role-name KarpenterNodeRole-${CLUSTER_NAME}
```

```bash
# --- Role 2: Controller Role (Karpenter itself uses this to call AWS APIs) ---
# This is what lets Karpenter actually launch and terminate EC2 instances.
# Without this, Karpenter installs but CANNOT create any nodes.

# Create the controller policy
cat > karpenter-controller-policy.json << EOF
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Action": [
        "ec2:RunInstances", "ec2:CreateLaunchTemplate",
        "ec2:CreateFleet", "ec2:DescribeInstances",
        "ec2:DescribeInstanceTypes", "ec2:DescribeSubnets",
        "ec2:DescribeSecurityGroups", "ec2:DescribeLaunchTemplates",
        "ec2:DescribeImages", "ec2:DescribeSpotPriceHistory",
        "ec2:TerminateInstances", "ec2:DeleteLaunchTemplate",
        "ec2:CreateTags"
      ],
      "Resource": "*"
    },
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Action": ["iam:PassRole"],
      "Resource": "arn:aws:iam::${ACCOUNT_ID}:role/KarpenterNodeRole-${CLUSTER_NAME}"
    },
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Action": ["ssm:GetParameter"],
      "Resource": "arn:aws:ssm:*:*:parameter/aws/service/*"
    },
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Action": ["pricing:GetProducts"],
      "Resource": "*"
    }
  ]
}
EOF

aws iam create-policy \
  --policy-name KarpenterControllerPolicy-${CLUSTER_NAME} \
  --policy-document file://karpenter-controller-policy.json

# Create the controller service account with IRSA
eksctl create iamserviceaccount \
  --cluster=${CLUSTER_NAME} \
  --region=${AWS_REGION} \
  --namespace=kube-system \
  --name=karpenter \
  --attach-policy-arn=arn:aws:iam::${ACCOUNT_ID}:policy/KarpenterControllerPolicy-${CLUSTER_NAME} \
  --approve

# Get the role ARN that was just created (needed for Helm install)
KARPENTER_IAM_ROLE_ARN=$(aws iam get-role \
  --role-name eksctl-${CLUSTER_NAME}-addon-iamserviceaccount-kube-system-karpenter-Role1 \
  --query Role.Arn --output text 2>/dev/null || \
  aws cloudformation describe-stacks \
  --query "Stacks[?contains(StackName,'karpenter')].Outputs[?OutputKey=='Role1Arn'].OutputValue" \
  --output text)
echo "Karpenter IAM Role ARN: $KARPENTER_IAM_ROLE_ARN"
```

**Step 3: Install Karpenter via Helm**
```bash
helm registry logout public.ecr.aws || true

helm upgrade --install karpenter oci://public.ecr.aws/karpenter/karpenter \
  --version 1.0.0 \
  --namespace kube-system \
  --set serviceAccount.create=false \
  --set serviceAccount.name=karpenter \
  --set settings.clusterName=${CLUSTER_NAME} \
  --set controller.resources.requests.cpu=1 \
  --set controller.resources.requests.memory=1Gi
```

> 💡 **Note:** Pin `--version 1.0.0` or check the latest at https://github.com/aws/karpenter/releases

**Step 4: Tell Karpenter what kind of nodes to create**

This is called a `NodePool` — it defines the rules for what nodes Karpenter can spin up:

```yaml
# nodepool.yaml
apiVersion: karpenter.sh/v1
kind: NodePool
metadata:
  name: default
spec:
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        cashify.in/lifecycle: spot    # your existing label convention
    spec:
      nodeClassRef:
        group: karpenter.k8s.aws
        kind: EC2NodeClass
        name: default
      requirements:
      - key: karpenter.sh/capacity-type
        operator: In
        values: ["spot", "on-demand"]
      - key: kubernetes.io/arch
        operator: In
        values: ["amd64"]
      - key: karpenter.k8s.aws/instance-category
        operator: In
        values: ["m", "c", "r"]       # memory/compute/general families
      - key: karpenter.k8s.aws/instance-generation
        operator: Gt
        values: ["2"]
  limits:
    cpu: 100                          # hard cap — prevents runaway scaling
  disruption:
    consolidationPolicy: WhenEmptyOrUnderutilized
    consolidateAfter: 30s            # remove idle nodes after 30s
---
apiVersion: karpenter.k8s.aws/v1
kind: EC2NodeClass
metadata:
  name: default
spec:
  amiFamily: AL2023
  role: KarpenterNodeRole-demo-test
  subnetSelectorTerms:
  - tags:
      karpenter.sh/discovery: demo-test
  securityGroupSelectorTerms:
  - tags:
      karpenter.sh/discovery: demo-test
```

```bash
kubectl apply -f nodepool.yaml
```

Now when you scale a deployment to 20 replicas and your nodes are full, Karpenter adds a new node automatically within ~60 seconds.

