## 3. Helm — Stop Copy-Pasting YAML

### The Problem It Solves

Right now, if you have 5 microservices, you have 5 copies of `deployment.yaml` and `service.yaml`. They all look almost the same — only the name, image, and port are different.

When you need to change something (like add a liveness probe), you edit 5 files. You miss one. Things break.

**Helm** lets you write the YAML once as a template with variables, and just pass different values for each service.

```
Without Helm:
  api-deployment.yaml      (copy-paste)
  auth-deployment.yaml     (copy-paste)
  payment-deployment.yaml  (copy-paste)
  → change one thing = edit 3 files, miss something, production breaks

With Helm:
  One chart template
  api/values.yaml      (just: name: api, port: 3001)
  auth/values.yaml     (just: name: auth, port: 3002)
  payment/values.yaml  (just: name: payment, port: 3003)
  → change one thing = edit template once, applies everywhere
```

### Install Helm

```bash
# macOS
brew install helm

# Linux
curl https://raw.githubusercontent.com/helm/helm/main/scripts/get-helm-3 | bash

# Verify
helm version
```

### Create a Helm Chart for Your Microservice

```bash
# This generates the chart folder structure
helm create my-microservice
```

It creates:
```
my-microservice/
├── Chart.yaml           ← chart name and version
├── values.yaml          ← your default config
└── templates/
    ├── deployment.yaml  ← template with {{ .Values.xxx }} variables
    ├── service.yaml
    └── ingress.yaml
```

**Edit `values.yaml`** — this is the only file you change per service:

```yaml
# values.yaml
replicaCount: 2

image:
  repository: 123456789.dkr.ecr.ap-south-1.amazonaws.com/my-microservice
  tag: latest
  pullPolicy: Always

service:
  type: ClusterIP
  port: 80
  targetPort: 3000

resources:
  requests:
    cpu: "100m"
    memory: "128Mi"
  limits:
    cpu: "250m"
    memory: "256Mi"
```

**Deploy with Helm:**

```bash
# First deploy
helm install my-microservice ./my-microservice

# Deploy with a custom value (override just one thing)
helm install my-microservice ./my-microservice --set replicaCount=5

# Update after a code change (new image pushed)
helm upgrade my-microservice ./my-microservice --set image.tag=v2.0.0

# See what changed before applying (dry run)
helm upgrade my-microservice ./my-microservice --dry-run

# Rollback to previous version
helm rollback my-microservice 1

# List all deployed charts
helm list

# Delete everything cleanly
helm uninstall my-microservice
```

### Deploy to Multiple Environments

This is where Helm shines. Create one `values.yaml` per environment:

```bash
my-microservice/
├── values.yaml            ← shared defaults
├── values-dev.yaml        ← dev overrides (1 replica, small instance)
├── values-staging.yaml    ← staging overrides (2 replicas)
└── values-prod.yaml       ← prod overrides (5 replicas, larger limits)
```

```bash
# Deploy to staging
helm upgrade --install my-microservice ./my-microservice \
  -f values.yaml \
  -f values-staging.yaml \
  --namespace staging

# Deploy to prod
helm upgrade --install my-microservice ./my-microservice \
  -f values.yaml \
  -f values-prod.yaml \
  --namespace prod
```

Same chart. Different values. No copy-paste.

