## 4. IRSA — Secure AWS Access for Pods

### The Problem It Solves

Your microservice might need to talk to AWS — read from S3, write to DynamoDB, send a message to SQS.

The **wrong way** (what many people do):

```yaml
# ❌ NEVER DO THIS — hardcoded keys in YAML
env:
- name: AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID
  value: "AKIAIOSFODNN7EXAMPLE"
- name: AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY
  value: "wJalrXUtnFEMI/K7MDENG/bPxRfiCYEXAMPLEKEY"
```

If someone gets access to your YAML or Git repo, they have full AWS access. No expiry. No limits.

**IRSA (IAM Roles for Service Accounts)** is the right way. A pod gets a temporary AWS identity automatically — no keys anywhere. The credentials rotate every hour by themselves.

```
Without IRSA:                    With IRSA:
  Pod has hardcoded keys    →      Pod has a Kubernetes service account
  Keys never expire         →      AWS gives it temp credentials (1hr TTL)
  Anyone with YAML = access →      Only that specific pod, in that namespace,
                                   can access exactly the S3 bucket you allowed
```

### Set Up IRSA Step by Step

**Step 1: Enable OIDC provider on your cluster** (one-time setup)

```bash
eksctl utils associate-iam-oidc-provider \
  --cluster demo-test \
  --region ap-south-1 \
  --approve
```

This links your EKS cluster to AWS IAM so pods can assume IAM roles.

**Step 2: Create an IAM policy** (what AWS actions your pod is allowed to do)

Example: allow the pod to read from one specific S3 bucket only.

```bash
cat > pod-s3-policy.json << EOF
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Action": ["s3:GetObject", "s3:ListBucket"],
      "Resource": [
        "arn:aws:s3:::cashify-app-data",
        "arn:aws:s3:::cashify-app-data/*"
      ]
    }
  ]
}
EOF

aws iam create-policy \
  --policy-name my-microservice-s3-policy \
  --policy-document file://pod-s3-policy.json
```

**Step 3: Create an IAM role + Kubernetes service account together**

```bash
ACCOUNT_ID=$(aws sts get-caller-identity --query Account --output text)

eksctl create iamserviceaccount \
  --cluster demo-test \
  --region ap-south-1 \
  --namespace default \
  --name my-microservice-sa \
  --attach-policy-arn arn:aws:iam::${ACCOUNT_ID}:policy/my-microservice-s3-policy \
  --approve
```

This creates:
- An IAM role in AWS
- A Kubernetes service account in your cluster
- A link between them via OIDC

**Step 4: Tell your Deployment to use this service account**

Add `serviceAccountName` to your deployment YAML:

```yaml
spec:
  template:
    spec:
      serviceAccountName: my-microservice-sa    # ← add this line
      containers:
      - name: my-microservice
        image: ...
        # No AWS keys anywhere. The pod gets credentials automatically.
```

```bash
kubectl apply -f deployment.yaml
```

Now your pod can call S3 without any hardcoded keys. AWS automatically gives it short-lived credentials, scoped to only what the policy allows. If the pod is deleted or the credentials leak, they expire within an hour anyway.

**Verify it works inside the pod:**
```bash
# Exec into a running pod
kubectl exec -it $(kubectl get pod -l app=my-microservice -o name | head -1) -- sh

# Inside the pod — this should work without any keys configured
aws s3 ls s3://cashify-app-data
```

